{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And Terah died in Haran.</b> Scripture has mentioned until now the generations of Shem and has not mentioned death at all. Why did it alter the order here and mention death in the case of Terah?" "<b>And our Rabbis</b>, of blessed memory, were roused to ask concerning this, for the matter was for the honor of Avram: had it not mentioned death in Terah, and Avram had left his land — as he was told, ״לך לך מארצך וממולדתך״ — people would murmur against him that he leaves his father and departs. And this is what they said: Scripture anticipated the death of Terah before the departure of Avram, so that people would not say that Avram did not fulfill the honor of father and mother, for he left him old and went on his way. Therefore Scripture needed to mention ״וימת תרח,״ unlike the rest of the generations of Shem. And Scripture called him dead because Terah was a wicked man, an idol worshipper, and the wicked in their lifetimes are called dead. And that which the Rabbis of blessed memory darshened — ״ואתה תבא אל אבותיך בשלום״ — this informed him that his father has a portion in the World to Come; this was in the merit of Avraham, but all the days of his life he was wicked and died in his wickedness. And perhaps because of this the נ of בחרן comes inverted, to allude that he died in his wickedness in the wrath of the Omnipresent, except that he has a portion in the World to Come in the merit of his son — and the fruit saved the tree." "<b>And from this</b> they said in the Midrash: all woods are fit for the altar except that of the olive and that of the vine, for the oil and the wine are offered at the altar — the fruits saved the trees. And likewise Avraham saved Terah his father, as it is said, ״ואתה תבא אל אבותיך בשלום.״" "<b>And it is possible</b> to give yet another reason for the inverted נ of חרן: for the inverted indicates תהו, and until here two thousand years of תהו are counted, and from Avraham onward are counted two thousand years of Torah, for he began to admonish the world with the light of Torah and with the proclamation of faith and unity." "<b>Behold,</b> this is among the perfections of the Torah and among the mysteries hidden in its letters. For this reason there came in the Torah inverted and suspended letters, and likewise curved and coiled ones, and likewise large and small ones, and likewise dotted and marked ones — so that from within them the fifty gates of understanding that were revealed to Moses may be disclosed. And furthermore, the Rabbis of blessed memory darshened: a son merits the father, but a father does not merit the son, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32): ״ואין מידי מציל״ — Avraham does not save Yishmael, nor Yitzhak save Esav. And concerning this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 17): ״עטרת זקנים בני בנים ותפארת בנים אבותם,״ for since the son merits the father, he used for sons the term עטרה, which is greater praise than תפארת, for they are a crown on the head of elders when those who issue from their loins are righteous servants of HaShem; and since the father does not merit the son, he used for him the term תפארת, for the righteous fathers are the splendor of sons when the sons hold fast to the deeds of their fathers, and the sons who guard their path in the uprightness of their conduct — the memory of their fathers' merit shall not cease from their seed; they shall be intertwined in virtues together and shall not be separated, and the stock of their lineage shall not die in the dust, they being the planting of HaShem, plantings resembling their Planter, as it is written (Isaiah 61): ״וקורא להם אילי הצדק מטע ה׳ להתפאר.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:53.244781495Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}